1
00:00:23,814 --> 00:00:35,744
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was wondering if we can talk about mindfulness of breathing in terms of what the Venerable Arita has said.

2
00:00:36,384 --> 00:00:41,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness of breathing is the mindfulness of breathing that he does in the suttas.

3
00:00:48,083 --> 00:00:56,444
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on one occasion, the Blessed One asked, monks, do you develop mindfulness of in-and-out breathing?

4
00:00:58,505 --> 00:01:04,506
[SPEAKER_00]: And when this was said, Venerable Arita replied to the Blessed One, I develop mindfulness of in-and-out breathing, Lord.

5
00:01:05,766 --> 00:01:07,806
[SPEAKER_00]: But how do you develop such mindfulness?

6
00:01:09,067 --> 00:01:16,148
[SPEAKER_00]: And Arita replies, having abandoned sensual desire for past sensual pleasures,

7
00:01:17,696 --> 00:01:34,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Having done away with sensual desire for future sensual pleasures, and having thoroughly subdued perceptions of resistance with regard to internal and external events, I breathe in mindfully and I breathe out mindfully.

8
00:01:35,851 --> 00:01:40,455
[SPEAKER_00]: So the Buddha says that there is that kind of... there is that mindfulness of breathing.

9
00:01:41,928 --> 00:01:51,236
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't say that there isn't, but as to how mindfulness of in-and-out breathing is brought in detail to its culmination, listen and pay close attention, I will speak.

10
00:01:52,237 --> 00:01:57,081
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it goes in the classic description in detail.

11
00:01:57,141 --> 00:01:57,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right.

12
00:01:58,542 --> 00:02:06,048
[SPEAKER_00]: But, so what I'm interested in is, is Arita's mindfulness of in-and-out, his description.

13
00:02:07,830 --> 00:02:08,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I think the

14
00:02:10,582 --> 00:02:15,463
[SPEAKER_01]: The bit to mention there is, I think, some people might think that Arita was wrong with something.

15
00:02:16,543 --> 00:02:21,384
[SPEAKER_01]: That what he described is not correct mindfulness of breathing, and then the Buddha gave him the right one.

16
00:02:21,564 --> 00:02:22,905
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not what the Buddha says.

17
00:02:23,085 --> 00:02:24,585
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, yeah, what you said is right.

18
00:02:24,965 --> 00:02:29,066
[SPEAKER_01]: It is mindfulness of breathing, but it's not complete.

19
00:02:30,006 --> 00:02:38,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Venerable Arita's mindfulness of breathing can certainly... well, certainly helped him free himself from sensuality, factually.

20
00:02:39,773 --> 00:02:44,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Because Buddha didn't say, oh, you're not free from sensuality and that's not mindfulness of breathing, which you described.

21
00:02:45,135 --> 00:02:51,158
[SPEAKER_01]: He just said, that's fine, but let me give you the detail that results in culmination of that practice.

22
00:02:51,678 --> 00:02:54,199
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just not enough for for arahantship.

23
00:02:54,419 --> 00:03:00,342
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, you need to go into a bit more detail, which then also makes you realize that whenever Arita described,

24
00:03:01,247 --> 00:03:03,589
[SPEAKER_01]: was... is sufficient to make you an anāgāmī.

25
00:03:04,149 --> 00:03:11,495
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you practice mindfulness of breathing, even in that simple sense that he described, which is, by the way, how every mindfulness of breathing formulation does start.

26
00:03:12,055 --> 00:03:14,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindful... knowingly he breathes in, knowingly he breathes out.

27
00:03:16,439 --> 00:03:17,560
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all you need to know.

28
00:03:18,946 --> 00:03:29,890
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you stick to that, as Venerable Arita did, you will lose... you will overcome any desire towards the sensual pleasures now, of the past, for the future.

29
00:03:30,571 --> 00:03:46,997
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you would have surmounted the entire domain of sensual craving and, most importantly, you would have completely subdued that patiga, resistance or aversion towards your own senses and so on, that we spoke in previous videos.

30
00:03:49,013 --> 00:03:54,774
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, obviously, as Venerable Arhat declared, enough for the five lower fetters to be destroyed.

31
00:03:56,955 --> 00:04:01,716
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for the complete Arahantship, then, the Buddha gives a further description of where you get...

32
00:04:03,976 --> 00:04:11,618
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to, like, give specific descriptions, because people are already way too much taking ānāpānasati to be a mechanical method.

33
00:04:12,855 --> 00:04:37,415
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why I think it's actually better if people follow Venerable Arita's instruction first, because it will prevent you from falling into that mindless... or whatever you've been doing, and force you to see things on the level of knowledge, of an enduring breath underneath your experience, like what we said before, regardless of the mind state, regardless of the emotional state, regardless of the physical state.

34
00:04:38,218 --> 00:04:43,441
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why mindfulness of breathing brings satipaṭṭhānas to fulfillment.

35
00:04:44,922 --> 00:04:47,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind state, emotional state, physical state.

36
00:04:50,746 --> 00:05:03,633
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start getting into these details, how I will breathe in, like this, and I'll do this to the body formation, and I'll do this to the body formation, that will all become apparent, if you become what Venerable Arita is there.

37
00:05:04,414 --> 00:05:05,995
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you overcome all the sensuality,

38
00:05:07,475 --> 00:05:11,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Subdue all the patiga you have towards your own senses.

39
00:05:11,281 --> 00:05:15,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Have no ill will towards anything or anyone in the world, past or future.

40
00:05:17,191 --> 00:05:19,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, follow the rest of ānāpānasati.

41
00:05:21,067 --> 00:05:27,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you will know exactly what it meant how gliding the body and experiencing joy while breathing in or breathing out.

42
00:05:27,691 --> 00:05:28,831
[SPEAKER_01]: You know exactly what that means.

43
00:05:29,592 --> 00:05:39,777
[SPEAKER_01]: But otherwise, people will be doing it with sensuality, with pretty much as something I shall do that will result in even greater pleasure for me, greater experience.

44
00:05:41,398 --> 00:05:49,983
[SPEAKER_01]: Any greatness of ānāpānasati on that level can only be done, can only be developed on the basis of complete surmounting of sensuality in the five lower fetters.

45
00:05:50,700 --> 00:05:51,540
[SPEAKER_01]: But, that's what I mean.

46
00:05:51,681 --> 00:05:53,141
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, it's a very good news.

47
00:05:53,742 --> 00:05:56,103
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to worry about every single little step.

48
00:05:56,823 --> 00:06:10,210
[SPEAKER_01]: If you cultivate and bring to fulfillment the knowledge of breathing in, breathing out, regardless of the posture, regardless of the emotional state, regardless of the mental state, regardless of the specific thoughts you have in, that is your anchor, that is underneath your experience, that is always enduring.

49
00:06:10,950 --> 00:06:12,671
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't lose sight of it.

50
00:06:13,031 --> 00:06:13,431
[SPEAKER_01]: The breath.

51
00:06:14,132 --> 00:06:18,434
[SPEAKER_01]: The knowledge of the breath being there, not on focusing on the physical side of it.

52
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:24,202
[SPEAKER_01]: You can focus on the physical side of it, if you want, while you know that it's underneath you, for what it is.

53
00:06:25,823 --> 00:06:29,305
[SPEAKER_01]: That's really what the anchor is.

54
00:06:29,325 --> 00:06:36,369
[SPEAKER_01]: So, using the the enduring breath, it's already happening to you, underneath your emotional states, or mental states, or physical states.

55
00:06:38,583 --> 00:06:43,806
[SPEAKER_01]: As an anchor, an investing effort to not lose the sight of it peripherally, to be aware.

56
00:06:44,366 --> 00:06:56,554
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to stop doing what I'm doing in order to breathe, but I can certainly not allow myself to be too absorbed in what I'm doing, so that I forget that I am breathing at the same time.

57
00:06:57,848 --> 00:07:07,316
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's learning how to not overly commit to your focusing attention, and actually learn how to stretch your mind to see, like, these both ends simultaneously enduring.

58
00:07:08,077 --> 00:07:15,163
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I would refer to, like, you know, you develop samādhi, the mind becomes pliable and stretchy, not like a hard, twisted, knotted lump.

59
00:07:19,505 --> 00:07:26,731
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in his description, he goes beyond sensual pleasures, sensuality.

60
00:07:27,732 --> 00:07:38,420
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's virtue, there's restraint, and he's putting up with the pressure, he's enduring the pressure, whether he's overcome the pressure or not.

61
00:07:38,961 --> 00:07:43,364
[SPEAKER_00]: But even if he's putting up with the pressure, not giving in to sensuality,

62
00:07:44,626 --> 00:07:53,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, he says, subduing those perceptions of resistance, of aversion.

63
00:07:53,789 --> 00:08:06,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and as you're saying, you know, so you give up all these sensual exploits, things calm down, and what you're left with is

64
00:08:08,467 --> 00:08:11,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Clearly seeing reality, the reality of your body.

65
00:08:12,610 --> 00:08:29,101
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, that reality will present itself first as this revulsion, as the suttas talk about, in the sense of you're going to experience almost like physical resistance, sickness, like a heroin addict withdrawing from the drugs or something.

66
00:08:29,141 --> 00:08:33,343
[SPEAKER_01]: You are withdrawing from the domain of the entire central being.

67
00:08:34,984 --> 00:08:37,066
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's going to be felt physically, but actually it's not.

68
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:44,265
[SPEAKER_01]: The physical aspect of it, the pain of that endurance, is your aversion towards it, towards the discomfort.

69
00:08:44,805 --> 00:08:48,648
[SPEAKER_01]: But discomfort in itself, it's actually quite bearable, if samādhi has been developed.

70
00:08:51,009 --> 00:09:02,238
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like, if a monk doesn't have the samādhi in that calming, in that proper sense, to use for the calming of the endurance, not to get rid of the endurance, or to place pleasure over it.

71
00:09:02,378 --> 00:09:06,101
[SPEAKER_01]: No, just to calm the aversion aspect of the same thing.

72
00:09:07,041 --> 00:09:12,784
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, mind is absolutely, can become absolutely boundless, limitless, in that sense.

73
00:09:12,924 --> 00:09:21,889
[SPEAKER_01]: As light as you can imagine, the most agreeable form of experience within the same endurance, because it was your aversion that was making it heavy.

74
00:09:23,148 --> 00:09:24,509
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's that's where the work is.

75
00:09:24,669 --> 00:09:26,010
[SPEAKER_01]: It's subduing the aversion.

76
00:09:26,390 --> 00:09:33,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing like psychologizing and trying to justify the purpose for endurance, or why you shouldn't do it, or you should do it, or why you should give in, or you shouldn't give in.

77
00:09:34,014 --> 00:09:36,895
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that means you're still revolving around, basically, rooted in aversion.

78
00:09:37,455 --> 00:09:37,575
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

79
00:09:37,595 --> 00:09:44,419
[SPEAKER_01]: The pure aversion that you have towards the experience of non-centrality, you have to abandon the aversion of it.

80
00:09:45,745 --> 00:09:49,407
[SPEAKER_01]: The aversion towards your own senses that are not engaging with with sensual distractions.

81
00:09:49,827 --> 00:09:52,409
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the sensual distractions are so powerful.

82
00:09:52,929 --> 00:09:56,771
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because the aversion underneath it, it's extremely strong.

83
00:09:57,912 --> 00:10:01,434
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only mean of escape from it is towards sense desires.

84
00:10:01,794 --> 00:10:04,376
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're saying that's your first thing.

85
00:10:04,736 --> 00:10:08,458
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're born, you feel, you experience the body.

86
00:10:08,698 --> 00:10:09,678
[SPEAKER_00]: And your first act.

87
00:10:09,999 --> 00:10:11,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what's meant by underlying tendencies.

88
00:10:12,380 --> 00:10:12,820
[SPEAKER_01]: They're tempted.

89
00:10:12,840 --> 00:10:13,360
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not there.

90
00:10:13,380 --> 00:10:15,201
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not... the baby is not doing anything.

91
00:10:15,221 --> 00:10:17,502
[SPEAKER_01]: The baby is not proliferating, choosing sensuality.

92
00:10:18,002 --> 00:10:22,905
[SPEAKER_01]: But the baby, its experience, its mind, is paired with the senses, with the five senses.

93
00:10:24,005 --> 00:10:26,646
[SPEAKER_01]: And they are uncomfortable.

94
00:10:27,967 --> 00:10:29,547
[SPEAKER_01]: However, subtly.

95
00:10:30,908 --> 00:10:35,010
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is nothing else there, except that discomfort for an undeveloped mind, for a newborn mind.

96
00:10:36,173 --> 00:10:43,118
[SPEAKER_01]: And what an unenlightened mind does, regardless of the type of discomfort it experiences, it wants to get rid of it.

97
00:10:44,279 --> 00:10:46,441
[SPEAKER_01]: So, naturally, on that fundamental level,

98
00:10:47,652 --> 00:10:54,336
[SPEAKER_01]: When the baby is experiencing the discomfort of its newborn body and senses, it's just naturally going in the direction towards objects.

99
00:10:54,876 --> 00:10:57,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Not that it chooses to, but the discomfort pushes it towards.

100
00:10:57,758 --> 00:11:12,747
[SPEAKER_01]: You say... And then everything else it starts encountering in the world, you know, the mother, breastfeeding, just becomes like a confirmation of that direction where it found safety from this discomfort that you yourself are, this unsafety.

101
00:11:13,627 --> 00:11:19,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're just left with the senses for what they are, five wild animals in the world, then you realize, that's what I am.

102
00:11:19,534 --> 00:11:20,595
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nothing else to me.

103
00:11:21,115 --> 00:11:23,678
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the reflections that Buddha would encourage to review this body.

104
00:11:23,738 --> 00:11:26,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to mechanically see what's anatomically made of.

105
00:11:26,981 --> 00:11:31,987
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just to see that even the images of yourself you have are images of this bag of skin.

106
00:11:33,377 --> 00:11:43,301
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's always, any memory you have, and if it involves this bag of skin, these five senses, these organs existing there, and that's very unsafe.

107
00:11:44,882 --> 00:11:47,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, other people have access to your senses more than you do.

108
00:11:49,104 --> 00:11:53,485
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you're also versed towards others as well, through the same process of resistance.

109
00:11:53,866 --> 00:11:59,548
[SPEAKER_00]: But that seeing form, or your body, or the six senses, or the elements for what they are,

110
00:12:00,883 --> 00:12:10,450
[SPEAKER_00]: It means that you see them as uncomfortable or pressured, pressured, weight, you know, burden.

111
00:12:10,490 --> 00:12:21,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you will see them, practically speaking, you will see them as a pressure, but actually they don't, they don't only, there is only pressure when there is, when you're seeing them with ignorance, which baby does, underlying tendency to avijja.

112
00:12:21,638 --> 00:12:26,822
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not, if there was no avijja in baby's mind, it wouldn't have been a baby, it wouldn't have been born.

113
00:12:27,870 --> 00:12:28,591
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole point.

114
00:12:29,031 --> 00:12:34,436
[SPEAKER_01]: So that pressure, practically, yes, that's how you're going to see them, but actually that pressure is completely gone in another hand.

115
00:12:36,037 --> 00:12:45,166
[SPEAKER_01]: So that pressure is the result of seeing them with... well, not clearly, with aversion or with any other thing that you have not abandoned.

116
00:12:46,497 --> 00:12:47,778
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you do see them clearly...

117
00:12:47,858 --> 00:13:03,193
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do see them clearly, and if you sustain the clarity, you will be unable to appropriate them, to own them, to even resist them in that sense, because even aversion towards them, it's kind of inconceivable, if you clarify it sufficiently enough.

118
00:13:03,834 --> 00:13:04,975
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the aversion will fade.

119
00:13:08,032 --> 00:13:15,374
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so, in terms of, okay, elements, sixth sense, body, there is a fundamental discomfort.

120
00:13:15,834 --> 00:13:16,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that you're averse to it.

121
00:13:17,034 --> 00:13:17,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

122
00:13:17,494 --> 00:13:18,995
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's a fundamental discomfort.

123
00:13:19,015 --> 00:13:19,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, to a degree.

124
00:13:19,455 --> 00:13:20,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Of the body.

125
00:13:20,095 --> 00:13:20,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

126
00:13:20,575 --> 00:13:21,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Being a body.

127
00:13:21,295 --> 00:13:21,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Of that form.

128
00:13:22,556 --> 00:13:32,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Form, like, what I said, what is the nature of rūpa is, or if you translate as form or matter, is, nature of form is to deform.

129
00:13:32,658 --> 00:13:32,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

130
00:13:33,648 --> 00:13:36,254
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... and that's... that's just how it is.

131
00:13:36,615 --> 00:13:37,597
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the nature of the body.

132
00:13:37,878 --> 00:13:38,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is...

133
00:13:38,238 --> 00:13:39,501
[SPEAKER_01]: The elements are constantly...

134
00:13:40,930 --> 00:13:44,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Changing, reshaping, and your body's doing the same.

135
00:13:44,091 --> 00:13:44,531
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, elements.

136
00:13:45,511 --> 00:13:47,551
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, that you cannot remove.

137
00:13:47,732 --> 00:13:50,992
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot just... and you can try and conceal your body, and that doesn't work.

138
00:13:51,232 --> 00:13:53,873
[SPEAKER_00]: You can try and control the elements, it doesn't work.

139
00:13:54,473 --> 00:14:01,014
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to kind of appease that, that creature, appease those elements.

140
00:14:01,134 --> 00:14:02,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Remember those, the vipers?

141
00:14:02,895 --> 00:14:05,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, the four great elements that you have to bathe and so on.

142
00:14:06,095 --> 00:14:09,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly, you appease them by removing your aversion towards them.

143
00:14:10,034 --> 00:14:13,656
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was the same, just you reminded me now, when you said the elements and so on.

144
00:14:14,456 --> 00:14:25,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Like when that, when a monk, or when that deva, I think, was talking to a monk or to the Buddha, he was about to reach the end of the world, like travels across the universe to find the end of the elements.

145
00:14:26,463 --> 00:14:30,305
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, you, you, doesn't matter how far you go, all you're going to find is elements.

146
00:14:31,866 --> 00:14:33,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Like they're infinite in that sense.

147
00:14:33,727 --> 00:14:39,050
[SPEAKER_01]: The ending of the elements is with this own body, when there is no more footing found in your consciousness for them.

148
00:14:40,322 --> 00:14:47,325
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when your consciousness cease... when you cease to appropriate them, when you cease to have lust towards them or aversion towards them, they will cease.

149
00:14:48,045 --> 00:14:49,786
[SPEAKER_01]: They will not pertain to you anymore.

150
00:14:51,166 --> 00:14:53,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, truly, you reach the end of the universe.

151
00:14:58,929 --> 00:14:59,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Arita is doing.

152
00:15:00,430 --> 00:15:03,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Subduing all perceptions of... How do you do that?

153
00:15:04,091 --> 00:15:05,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by... by...

154
00:15:09,589 --> 00:15:16,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoroughly developing sense restraint, and then being... guarding the doors of the sense faculties.

155
00:15:17,711 --> 00:15:25,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean refusing to see things or hear things, but means never doing so without being on guard.

156
00:15:25,552 --> 00:15:27,012
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's often where people conflate.

157
00:15:29,073 --> 00:15:31,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, they think guarding the sense faculties means saying no to everything.

158
00:15:31,673 --> 00:15:35,174
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not look at anything, hear anything, I will avoid, I will run away if I start hearing things.

159
00:15:35,234 --> 00:15:35,674
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

160
00:15:36,637 --> 00:15:41,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Guarding means you have to see, hear, smell, taste, touch things.

161
00:15:41,460 --> 00:15:51,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Keeping the the the close eye at the doorways of your senses, so guarding the sense faculties, that's the work.

162
00:15:53,487 --> 00:15:56,429
[SPEAKER_01]: So now we're talking, you're, you know, I'm aware of what I'm seeing and so on.

163
00:15:56,449 --> 00:16:02,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Say I hear something behind, I don't just look without thinking.

164
00:16:04,490 --> 00:16:14,933
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be aware of your... like, if you want to look, of your faculty that you're looking in case of what you see might induce lust in you or aversion in you.

165
00:16:14,973 --> 00:16:15,733
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what Buddha said.

166
00:16:16,154 --> 00:16:27,377
[SPEAKER_01]: He keeps the... the guard of his... he guards his sense faculties and then he doesn't give attention to any signs and features that arise within that.

167
00:16:27,497 --> 00:16:33,659
[SPEAKER_01]: So, say, I heard something and I have an image of what that might be and based on that image I'll decide whether I should look or not.

168
00:16:36,630 --> 00:16:43,233
[SPEAKER_01]: And if I have to look, then I'll be extra careful, so that when I do look, unwholesome states don't arise in me.

169
00:16:43,794 --> 00:16:48,676
[SPEAKER_01]: So it can sound like, oh, I have to do a lot, but that's only because you haven't been guarding the senses properly.

170
00:16:49,656 --> 00:16:53,618
[SPEAKER_01]: People, as I said, people confuse guarding with just refusing to see, refusing to look.

171
00:16:53,638 --> 00:16:53,838
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

172
00:16:54,199 --> 00:16:58,661
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to allow some degree of looking and seeing and smelling and tasting and touching.

173
00:16:59,461 --> 00:17:01,962
[SPEAKER_01]: But you just always want to be on guard in regard to that.

174
00:17:02,423 --> 00:17:03,803
[SPEAKER_00]: But it might... because if you were to

175
00:17:05,524 --> 00:17:16,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Kind of crush the body, keep it blind, not let it hear anything, not let it eat what, you know, basic amount of, little bit of food, hurt the body, self-mortify it.

176
00:17:17,573 --> 00:17:22,797
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be agitated, just as agitated as it would be if you were to overindulge.

177
00:17:22,857 --> 00:17:23,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

178
00:17:23,978 --> 00:17:25,840
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to calm these wild dogs?

179
00:17:26,945 --> 00:17:32,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you won't do by giving them what they want, all the time, and you won't do by beating them and starving them either.

180
00:17:33,008 --> 00:17:37,570
[SPEAKER_01]: It will make them even wilder, more often than not, that level of torture.

181
00:17:38,210 --> 00:17:51,276
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to balance it, give them when needed, not unguarded, so they don't eat all of it, or as much as they want, but, you know, doesn't mean don't give them anything at all.

182
00:17:51,916 --> 00:17:54,878
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I also read about this practice, eh...

183
00:17:58,338 --> 00:18:02,903
[SPEAKER_00]: It's called the element calming meditation.

184
00:18:03,643 --> 00:18:10,330
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for example, I would say earth samma meditation.

185
00:18:12,440 --> 00:18:16,261
[SPEAKER_00]: There is nothing that you do that agitates this form, these elements, this body.

186
00:18:29,167 --> 00:18:38,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the practice of sammā, not sammā as in the right, sammā as in tuned to the right note.

187
00:18:38,456 --> 00:18:39,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is that practice?

188
00:18:39,877 --> 00:18:45,903
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in the... when he's described... when he's teaching Rāhula how to practice mindfulness of breathing.

189
00:18:46,023 --> 00:18:46,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

190
00:18:46,783 --> 00:18:51,248
[SPEAKER_00]: And he's teaching him how to subdue that patīgā, really, by calming

191
00:18:51,788 --> 00:18:54,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, that's the suitable mindfulness of breathing, isn't it, wasn't it?

192
00:18:54,111 --> 00:18:54,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

193
00:18:54,531 --> 00:18:54,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right.

194
00:18:54,872 --> 00:18:58,176
[SPEAKER_00]: How to do mindfulness of being in regard to the elements.

195
00:18:58,376 --> 00:19:00,579
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not doing anything that agitates.

196
00:19:00,739 --> 00:19:00,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

197
00:19:01,440 --> 00:19:04,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically speaking, you can...

198
00:19:05,573 --> 00:19:07,074
[SPEAKER_01]: People can see that for themselves.

199
00:19:07,754 --> 00:19:12,256
[SPEAKER_01]: For example, when the body is fatigued and you're tired, you're irritated.

200
00:19:12,396 --> 00:19:12,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Irritable.

201
00:19:13,256 --> 00:19:13,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Irritated.

202
00:19:14,296 --> 00:19:27,321
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no reason for you, for example, to be irritated when the body is fatigued, when the body is heavy, tired, where the solidity aspect, where the element of earth is felt more heavier, dragging you down or whatever else.

203
00:19:28,602 --> 00:19:29,062
[SPEAKER_01]: But you are.

204
00:19:30,102 --> 00:19:32,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind is not developed in regard to the body.

205
00:19:34,551 --> 00:19:38,880
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you can't just... you can't just count these elements abstractly.

206
00:19:38,920 --> 00:19:41,745
[SPEAKER_01]: You can try, but that's not what the elements are.

207
00:19:41,786 --> 00:19:43,890
[SPEAKER_01]: You are, like, the base of elements, the body.

208
00:19:44,010 --> 00:19:44,912
[SPEAKER_01]: They found a footing.

209
00:19:45,756 --> 00:19:47,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Through your own misconceiving and so on.

210
00:19:48,017 --> 00:20:03,601
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when these elements change, when your body... and I don't mean just change in terms of aging or something, but even, yeah, that sometimes your body feels light, sometimes feels heavy, sometimes feels tired, sometimes feels energized, sometimes feels sleepy, sometimes awake, sometimes burning, sometimes cold.

211
00:20:04,121 --> 00:20:07,082
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those... that's what elements are.

212
00:20:07,862 --> 00:20:13,784
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you train in regard to that, not in regard to what you perceive elements to be on account of your body, like all elements and elements, you know.

213
00:20:14,464 --> 00:20:17,646
[SPEAKER_01]: That's you, abstractly now, just trying to go through the motions of that contemplation.

214
00:20:17,666 --> 00:20:17,886
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

215
00:20:18,346 --> 00:20:26,031
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mindfully breathing in, knowingly breathing in, knowingly breathing out, underneath this body, what is that body that underneath with there is a breath?

216
00:20:26,611 --> 00:20:27,312
[SPEAKER_01]: How is it felt?

217
00:20:28,510 --> 00:20:29,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it tired?

218
00:20:29,451 --> 00:20:30,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it heavy?

219
00:20:30,111 --> 00:20:30,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it light?

220
00:20:30,832 --> 00:20:31,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it aching?

221
00:20:32,133 --> 00:20:33,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are the elements.

222
00:20:33,554 --> 00:20:37,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are literally the things you need to develop your mind in regard to.

223
00:20:37,417 --> 00:20:38,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Because tired?

224
00:20:39,278 --> 00:20:40,078
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm averse to that.

225
00:20:40,419 --> 00:20:40,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Aching?

226
00:20:40,939 --> 00:20:41,820
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm averse to that.

227
00:20:42,080 --> 00:20:42,400
[SPEAKER_00]: Cold?

228
00:20:42,660 --> 00:20:44,682
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm averse to that.

229
00:20:44,902 --> 00:20:45,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Restless?

230
00:20:45,743 --> 00:20:46,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Too much?

231
00:20:46,203 --> 00:20:47,124
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm averse to that.

232
00:20:47,244 --> 00:20:48,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that's where aversion is.

233
00:20:49,637 --> 00:20:53,881
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to me and this body, or my point of view in regard to this body.

234
00:20:54,341 --> 00:20:59,206
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what would have meant, for as long as you're not developed in the body, you will be attracted to sensuality.

235
00:21:01,688 --> 00:21:03,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Not developed in the body, meaning?

236
00:21:03,049 --> 00:21:06,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, meaning you, in regard to the body, are not developed.

237
00:21:06,532 --> 00:21:14,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you still have aversion in regard to the discomfort, and by extension of the same principle, you're going to have lust when you experience comfort.

238
00:21:15,814 --> 00:21:19,635
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's actually in regard to the way the elements are at the time.

239
00:21:20,875 --> 00:21:26,297
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the Buddha said there are all those asuras to be abandoned simply by enduring them correctly.

240
00:21:27,817 --> 00:21:39,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Heat, cold, insects, discomfort, burning sun and so on, words of others, abuse of others, all of that is on the level of you, your body being factually assailed, because that's what body is, devoured.

241
00:21:42,008 --> 00:21:45,793
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, they said even the five senses are attacked by agreeable and disagreeable.

242
00:21:46,714 --> 00:21:48,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Ears are attacked by agreeable and disagreeable sounds.

243
00:21:49,037 --> 00:21:49,879
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the nature of it.

244
00:21:50,700 --> 00:21:51,881
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just between them.

245
00:21:52,182 --> 00:21:53,003
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not your problem.

246
00:21:53,787 --> 00:21:56,968
[SPEAKER_01]: But your problem is to have lust towards agreeable and aversion towards disagreeable.

247
00:21:57,628 --> 00:21:58,128
[SPEAKER_01]: So you don't.

248
00:21:58,489 --> 00:22:03,230
[SPEAKER_01]: So you remove your lust towards it, you remove your aversion towards it, and then it won't be a problem.

249
00:22:03,530 --> 00:22:08,712
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the arahant, who says everything that, you know, all the taints to be removed, I have removed.

250
00:22:08,732 --> 00:22:10,493
[SPEAKER_01]: The being, the centrality, the ignorance.

251
00:22:11,513 --> 00:22:14,974
[SPEAKER_01]: All that's left is the disturbances that can arise on account of this body until it breaks apart.

252
00:22:15,634 --> 00:22:17,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Like these natural level of disturbances.

253
00:22:20,891 --> 00:22:26,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's... you have to be careful if you want to be doing... calming the elements, to not just go into visualizations and so on.

254
00:22:27,532 --> 00:22:30,813
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't... you can't calm the elements on your own terms, because they cannot be yours.

255
00:22:31,333 --> 00:22:33,813
[SPEAKER_01]: You calm them by finding what state they are in now.

256
00:22:35,074 --> 00:22:36,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Your body... your body is your element.

257
00:22:38,034 --> 00:22:42,175
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's often the same... use the word... same word... rūpa for the body, rūpa for the elements.

258
00:22:43,055 --> 00:22:46,956
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your practical experience of the elements.

259
00:22:49,362 --> 00:22:54,908
[SPEAKER_00]: So, bringing the elements to equilibrium, what does that mean?

260
00:22:56,190 --> 00:22:57,772
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what we're talking about now.

261
00:22:57,812 --> 00:22:58,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

262
00:22:58,192 --> 00:22:59,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it means that.

263
00:23:01,496 --> 00:23:03,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, you might start with the fatigue.

264
00:23:04,779 --> 00:23:07,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Naturally, to that extent, that's how elements present themselves.

265
00:23:07,402 --> 00:23:09,565
[SPEAKER_01]: But through your unrestrained senses,

266
00:23:10,324 --> 00:23:12,165
[SPEAKER_01]: You get tired even more unnecessarily.

267
00:23:12,426 --> 00:23:18,030
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you sent the elements even further out of that, what they were naturally arisen.

268
00:23:18,730 --> 00:23:19,911
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... you disturb them.

269
00:23:20,391 --> 00:23:21,592
[SPEAKER_01]: You make it even worse.

270
00:23:22,092 --> 00:23:25,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Or being too averse towards it, you feel even tired than you actually are.

271
00:23:25,495 --> 00:23:28,277
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can even come to the point that you're so averse now,

272
00:23:29,518 --> 00:23:35,381
[SPEAKER_01]: That physically, you cease to be tired, yet mentally, you're just knackered.

273
00:23:35,401 --> 00:23:36,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of your aversion.

274
00:23:36,802 --> 00:23:37,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because the elements with that.

275
00:23:37,883 --> 00:23:40,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Bringing them back to their natural state.

276
00:23:40,184 --> 00:23:44,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing them for what they are arisen and not interfering with it and enduring it without aversion.

277
00:23:44,907 --> 00:23:46,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Removing aversion towards the endurance of them.

278
00:23:46,808 --> 00:23:47,909
[SPEAKER_01]: That's their natural state.

279
00:23:48,569 --> 00:23:52,831
[SPEAKER_00]: But even though their natural state is not peace and bliss and lovely and wonderful.

280
00:23:53,132 --> 00:23:54,252
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not dukkha either.

281
00:23:54,312 --> 00:23:55,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

282
00:23:55,213 --> 00:23:56,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Dukkha is your interference.

283
00:23:56,413 --> 00:23:57,274
[SPEAKER_01]: Dukkha is your aversion.

284
00:23:57,314 --> 00:23:57,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Dukkha is your lust.

285
00:23:57,954 --> 00:23:58,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Dukkha is your craving.

286
00:23:59,880 --> 00:24:01,702
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that's important to understand.

287
00:24:01,742 --> 00:24:06,106
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like natural state of the elements is this, you know, blissful mystical realm.

288
00:24:06,587 --> 00:24:06,727
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

289
00:24:07,087 --> 00:24:07,548
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm tired.

290
00:24:07,628 --> 00:24:10,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Naturally, the body is tired, didn't eat enough, worked too much.

291
00:24:11,111 --> 00:24:14,294
[SPEAKER_01]: That is a currently natural state of these elements.

292
00:24:14,354 --> 00:24:17,918
[SPEAKER_01]: All I need to do is not interfere with it and endure it mindfully.

293
00:24:19,248 --> 00:24:24,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you're seeing the natural discomfort, the natural state.

294
00:24:24,655 --> 00:24:24,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

295
00:24:25,115 --> 00:24:26,457
[SPEAKER_00]: The natural uncomfortable state.

296
00:24:26,477 --> 00:24:30,342
[SPEAKER_01]: The natural uncomfortable state, which is not dukkha, but it's factually not nice.

297
00:24:30,542 --> 00:24:35,107
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you can... then a person can see the asubha there.

298
00:24:37,669 --> 00:24:39,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it becomes obvious for what it is.

299
00:24:39,390 --> 00:24:41,731
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to be making effort to see it.

300
00:24:41,771 --> 00:24:43,011
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize it for what it is.

301
00:24:43,071 --> 00:24:45,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is not beautiful.

302
00:24:45,132 --> 00:24:50,495
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not worth... because it's... well, in itself, in its natural state, it's a burden.

303
00:24:50,715 --> 00:24:52,035
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a weight.

304
00:24:53,476 --> 00:24:56,157
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already not nice, not agreeable.

305
00:24:58,498 --> 00:25:00,159
[SPEAKER_00]: But also now, if you had to stop there,

306
00:25:01,148 --> 00:25:01,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop what?

307
00:25:01,829 --> 00:25:04,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you could be quite disgusted with that.

308
00:25:05,351 --> 00:25:05,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

309
00:25:05,932 --> 00:25:13,098
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... and so you... if you are quite disgusted with it, means your aversion is taking over, not the natural state.

310
00:25:13,578 --> 00:25:16,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the natural state has no aversion, nor lust.

311
00:25:17,101 --> 00:25:19,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, here you are full of aversion towards that.

312
00:25:19,503 --> 00:25:21,765
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the way he gave ānāpānasati.

313
00:25:22,245 --> 00:25:28,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Because some monks got too averse, thinking, oh, this body is truly like that, and they couldn't bear it.

314
00:25:28,960 --> 00:25:29,621
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, no.

315
00:25:29,661 --> 00:25:30,982
[SPEAKER_01]: If you calm your mind, you realize, no.

316
00:25:31,543 --> 00:25:33,405
[SPEAKER_01]: The body is uncomfortable to that extent.

317
00:25:34,667 --> 00:25:35,047
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

318
00:25:35,327 --> 00:25:36,208
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where it ends.

319
00:25:36,348 --> 00:25:39,011
[SPEAKER_01]: So, arahant-siddhi-nibbāna, here and now, is pleasant.

320
00:25:39,872 --> 00:25:40,793
[SPEAKER_01]: It's very pleasant.

321
00:25:41,995 --> 00:25:48,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Arahant... arahant's recognition of that discomfortable body and its clarity is the most pleasant experience you can imagine.

322
00:25:48,883 --> 00:25:49,924
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the enlightenment.

323
00:25:52,503 --> 00:26:07,327
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it's too disgusting, means, okay, so you did probably remove that essential perception sufficiently, but now you do need to remove your aversion of it as well, because that's not nibbāna, that's not going to be nibbāna.

324
00:26:07,347 --> 00:26:08,927
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the work there, which...

325
00:26:09,927 --> 00:26:12,528
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm jumping... And that's exactly where Venerable Arita said that.

326
00:26:13,248 --> 00:26:22,775
[SPEAKER_01]: First, it was the sensuality abandoned, and what he was left was the disgust and aversion towards the body, the senses, and then he subdued that, and now he's just mindfully breathing in, breathing out.

327
00:26:22,875 --> 00:26:31,061
[SPEAKER_00]: And in another sutta, I think this is where it goes into that what subduing of perceptions of patiga is.

328
00:26:32,142 --> 00:26:34,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Subduing that aversion.

329
00:26:34,304 --> 00:26:38,047
[SPEAKER_00]: In the Rahula sutta, where the Buddha is teaching him mindfulness of breathing,

330
00:26:38,987 --> 00:26:49,112
[SPEAKER_00]: He goes, I think, from the asubha, realizing the asubha there, then practicing brahma-vihāra, which culminates in

331
00:26:50,560 --> 00:26:54,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Upeka, which is for the overcoming of all patiga.

332
00:26:55,064 --> 00:26:55,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure.

333
00:26:55,745 --> 00:26:56,506
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the enlightenment.

334
00:26:56,666 --> 00:27:00,470
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's metta, karuṇa, contentment with the whole thing, mudita.

335
00:27:00,650 --> 00:27:02,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, because you don't need to be averse.

336
00:27:02,432 --> 00:27:03,072
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm saying.

337
00:27:03,212 --> 00:27:05,495
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize aversion is coming from me completely, unnecessarily.

338
00:27:05,815 --> 00:27:09,298
[SPEAKER_01]: If I remove aversion, this discomfort will be blissful.

339
00:27:10,019 --> 00:27:13,703
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, oh, and that's already seen the mind of friendliness, already.

340
00:27:15,389 --> 00:27:32,008
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so that's the... again, Brahmavihāras, another misconceived visualization practice, starts with a thorough, thorough, thorough, thorough watchfulness guarding of the sense doors, sense restraint, and then that aversion you're left with, of that endurance for what it is, you just...

341
00:27:32,028 --> 00:27:32,869
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll be friendly to it.

342
00:27:33,830 --> 00:27:34,912
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you start with yourself.

343
00:27:35,944 --> 00:27:40,167
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if through that state you think of others, you'll be friendly to anything, internal or external.

344
00:27:40,988 --> 00:27:45,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Any shape or form, any perception of a version that appears, I'll be friendly to it.

345
00:27:45,811 --> 00:27:47,933
[SPEAKER_01]: I will sustain the mind of friendliness.

346
00:27:48,493 --> 00:27:49,334
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all.

347
00:27:49,414 --> 00:27:50,194
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll protect that.

348
00:27:50,675 --> 00:27:56,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything that comes to it and goes internally, externally, internally and externally, I shall sustain the mind of friendliness.

349
00:27:56,859 --> 00:27:57,840
[SPEAKER_01]: That's my concern.

350
00:27:57,960 --> 00:28:03,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to spread love to you, just to not lose the mind of friendliness in regard to any patīgā that comes my way.

351
00:28:05,185 --> 00:28:10,187
[SPEAKER_01]: And whenever you search the suttas, you will see that how to deal with patīgā, loving-kindness, metta.

352
00:28:10,728 --> 00:28:12,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, essentially.

353
00:28:12,208 --> 00:28:17,991
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not like... Sure, ill-will towards others is a form of patīgā, but that's not patīgā.

354
00:28:18,931 --> 00:28:21,172
[SPEAKER_00]: You must have been well restrained before that.

355
00:28:21,352 --> 00:28:25,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mind should have been withdrawn if you're particularly concerned with hating this person, that person.

356
00:28:26,074 --> 00:28:28,616
[SPEAKER_01]: To do brahmā-vihāra properly, that would culminate in upekka.

357
00:28:30,256 --> 00:28:31,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like, you... the monk...

358
00:28:33,005 --> 00:28:47,699
[SPEAKER_01]: The diligent monk, sense-restraint, virtue-sense-restraint, guards the sense-doors, then he abandons, he doesn't cultivate, he doesn't welcome thoughts of sensuality, of sensual desires, doesn't entertain, doesn't psychologize why I hate or why I have these thoughts.

359
00:28:47,719 --> 00:28:47,859
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

360
00:28:47,999 --> 00:28:51,263
[SPEAKER_01]: He simply does not give any attention to those thoughts.

361
00:28:52,313 --> 00:28:53,133
[SPEAKER_01]: until they're subdued.

362
00:28:53,254 --> 00:28:57,336
[SPEAKER_01]: So, thoughts of sensuality, thoughts of cruelty, harm, ill-will, he's subdued.

363
00:28:57,676 --> 00:29:10,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, thoughts about his reputation, concern about his reputation, thoughts about his his home country or clansmen, like these kind of higher extensions of self that everybody carries, you know, without as hard as see me and know what I think.

364
00:29:11,083 --> 00:29:12,264
[SPEAKER_01]: He subdues that as well.

365
00:29:12,304 --> 00:29:14,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Not how long he justifies it or he thinks about it.

366
00:29:14,525 --> 00:29:15,186
[SPEAKER_01]: He subdues that.

367
00:29:15,786 --> 00:29:17,427
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he refuses to engage with these thoughts.

368
00:29:18,207 --> 00:29:20,829
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, what he's left with is the nature of things.

369
00:29:21,987 --> 00:29:23,208
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another sutta that says that.

370
00:29:24,109 --> 00:29:26,391
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like all these things will fall off as... No.

371
00:29:26,531 --> 00:29:29,454
[SPEAKER_01]: He makes the effort to not engage with them, because they'll try to hook you, you know.

372
00:29:29,474 --> 00:29:30,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoughts of centrality won't let you think about it.

373
00:29:31,075 --> 00:29:37,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Even in terms of asubha, you still accept centrality as a desire of a fact, and now try to get rid of it by thinking asubha.

374
00:29:38,322 --> 00:29:42,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoughts of cruelty will try to hook you to take them, so to practice your brahmavihāra.

375
00:29:43,406 --> 00:29:44,666
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, try to get rid of it.

376
00:29:44,766 --> 00:29:45,747
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not brahmahāra.

377
00:29:46,407 --> 00:29:51,249
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to be left with the clarity of that endurance that you're inevitably going to be averse to.

378
00:29:51,309 --> 00:29:51,929
[SPEAKER_01]: How much to?

379
00:29:51,969 --> 00:29:55,450
[SPEAKER_01]: That depends on how much sensuality you had carried from before.

380
00:29:55,690 --> 00:30:01,292
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, ah, it's the pure patīgā of this experience, internally, externally.

381
00:30:01,772 --> 00:30:03,853
[SPEAKER_01]: that I should just be friendly towards.

382
00:30:04,353 --> 00:30:08,454
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're friendly to the patīgā of your senses, you will be friendly to any patīgā that comes through it.

383
00:30:08,894 --> 00:30:17,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if people come and beat you up and abuse you, you cannot hate, because you cannot abandon the doorway of friendliness towards this, through which all of this manifests for you.

384
00:30:18,616 --> 00:30:23,057
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why truly, like metta, practice will free you from all the patīgā and result in upeka.

385
00:30:26,850 --> 00:30:37,242
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it's on that level, through sense restraint and watchfulness and guarding of the sense doors, you could just decide to be friendly towards this aversion.

386
00:30:37,462 --> 00:30:40,526
[SPEAKER_01]: And sustain the friendliness regardless of the aversion.

387
00:30:40,606 --> 00:30:42,669
[SPEAKER_01]: And aversion will be subdued.

388
00:30:42,689 --> 00:30:46,954
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, if you choose to sustain the knowledge of breathing,

389
00:30:48,183 --> 00:30:53,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Underneath your experience, so to speak, breathing in, breathing out, your thoughts will be subdued.

390
00:30:54,166 --> 00:30:55,087
[SPEAKER_01]: They will be calmed down.

391
00:30:55,367 --> 00:30:56,968
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to... How do I...

392
00:30:57,348 --> 00:31:10,396
[SPEAKER_01]: If I stick to this, knowledge that I'm breathing in, breathing out, while it is happening, or whatever else, knowledge that breath is underneath there, happening to me, in and out, in and out, and I don't completely lose the sight of it, thoughts will be subdued.

393
00:31:10,596 --> 00:31:12,377
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just the natural outcome of it.

394
00:31:12,960 --> 00:31:13,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

395
00:31:13,500 --> 00:31:20,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Sustain the friendliness towards yourself, in that sense of having been fully restrained, experiencing patiṅga of the sense restrained.

396
00:31:20,967 --> 00:31:24,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Just friendly towards that, nothing else, and patiṅga will subdue.

397
00:31:24,670 --> 00:31:39,463
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, in the Vahula Sutta, again, that I was mentioning, after that subduing of patiṅga, he then says, developed anicca, sañña.

398
00:31:41,185 --> 00:31:44,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's seeing things clearly.

399
00:31:45,127 --> 00:31:50,390
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be seeing the clear, true meaning of that and nature of that thing, because you're too averse towards it.

400
00:31:51,230 --> 00:31:52,871
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you subdue your aversion.

401
00:31:52,951 --> 00:31:57,153
[SPEAKER_01]: What you're left with is neither averse, certainly not, no central desire.

402
00:31:57,733 --> 00:31:59,054
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, oh, let me make the effort and see.

403
00:31:59,114 --> 00:32:04,016
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a reason on its own, inaccessible, not mine, as it is described.

404
00:32:04,036 --> 00:32:05,377
[SPEAKER_01]: But the clarity is there.

405
00:32:05,457 --> 00:32:05,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

406
00:32:05,917 --> 00:32:09,299
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of that clarity, that you're not interfering with your aversion now,

407
00:32:10,152 --> 00:32:20,055
[SPEAKER_01]: You get to specify aspects of it that you want to see, such as anicca, dukkha, and then the experience is fully going to be cessation of all traces of conceit.

408
00:32:20,256 --> 00:32:24,777
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha actually, after that, then he starts to teach the mindfulness of breathing.

409
00:32:24,797 --> 00:32:26,178
[SPEAKER_01]: The rest of the mindfulness of breathing, yeah.

410
00:32:26,878 --> 00:32:27,258
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

411
00:32:27,278 --> 00:32:28,358
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the fulfillment.

412
00:32:28,398 --> 00:32:35,841
[SPEAKER_01]: If you just then stick to that as a practice, if there are any traces of any appropriation, of any ignorance left, that you are kind of unaware of,

413
00:32:37,010 --> 00:32:40,374
[SPEAKER_01]: That will also disappear if you stick to that practice of that right order.

414
00:32:40,474 --> 00:32:45,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Breathing in, in that impersonal sense, being unable to experience aversion anymore, because you have subdued it.

415
00:32:46,322 --> 00:32:50,487
[SPEAKER_01]: No sensual desire and certainly no appropriation, because then perception for Nietzsche has been fully developed.

416
00:32:52,440 --> 00:33:01,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there were, you know, many, like, like, anāgāmīs in the case, they had so, like, little traces of bhāvīja left, that even the Buddha could not specifically say where and how.

417
00:33:01,688 --> 00:33:08,054
[SPEAKER_01]: He would just say, if there is, there might be some residue, and if there is, there will be, you know, non-return and so on.

418
00:33:09,377 --> 00:33:17,123
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, like, even if you think you're an arahant, complete, finished, just stick to that practice, because it's pleasant here and now.

419
00:33:17,163 --> 00:33:21,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Cultivate ānāpānasati to that fulfillment, as he said to Venerable Arita.

420
00:33:22,507 --> 00:33:30,353
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, eh... if there were any traces, that would be... that would have been dealt with, by sticking to that practice of calming correctly.

421
00:33:32,958 --> 00:33:34,300
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a win-win, in other words.

422
00:33:34,861 --> 00:33:43,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you were... even if you're an arahant, you win because it's a pleasant abiding here and now, and if you're not an arahant, you win because it's a pleasant abiding here and now, and it frees you from anything that was preventing you to be an arahant.

423
00:33:45,955 --> 00:33:49,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but there's no breathing there, all that work.

424
00:33:50,137 --> 00:33:52,739
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, where's the breathing?

425
00:33:52,759 --> 00:33:53,859
[SPEAKER_00]: Where's the breathing that you...

426
00:33:55,140 --> 00:34:00,383
[SPEAKER_00]: There's also other work that you're doing, you know, you're calming the fatigue, contemplating this...

427
00:34:00,403 --> 00:34:04,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Breathing is that just peripheral recollection, the knowledge of the breath enduring underneath you.

428
00:34:05,005 --> 00:34:12,509
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically the reference point that prevents you from being... to engage from your personal point of view, which you contemplate in.

429
00:34:14,343 --> 00:34:22,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're rooted in a naturally occurring breath underneath your experience, breathing in, breathing out, and refuse to give up... Give that basis up, basically.

430
00:34:22,212 --> 00:34:25,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Refuse to give... and that's already an impersonal reference point.

431
00:34:25,275 --> 00:34:26,696
[SPEAKER_00]: That impersonal reference point.

432
00:34:26,917 --> 00:34:32,263
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you... That's why, eventually, if you stick to it, everything else will be seen through the impersonal reference point.

433
00:34:32,999 --> 00:34:33,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Not mine.

434
00:34:33,980 --> 00:34:35,161
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what not mine is.

435
00:34:35,201 --> 00:34:35,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Impersonal.

436
00:34:35,642 --> 00:34:37,984
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you contemplate the elements, when you contemplate the senses...

437
00:34:38,004 --> 00:34:41,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Through the impersonal basis of breathing and during there, you're contemplating it rightly.

438
00:34:42,850 --> 00:34:48,656
[SPEAKER_00]: On that... through that impersonal basis of that thing which is so personal, so... Sure.

439
00:34:48,676 --> 00:34:49,197
[SPEAKER_00]: So close to you.

440
00:34:49,217 --> 00:34:49,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

441
00:34:49,357 --> 00:34:50,057
[SPEAKER_01]: So close to you.

442
00:34:50,077 --> 00:34:50,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

443
00:34:50,418 --> 00:34:53,081
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I'd say it's a useful and suitable reference point.

444
00:34:53,883 --> 00:35:06,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you could take other reference points, but they would be exactly... sooner or later you end up appropriating them, so then it ceases to be an impersonal reference point through which you get to see all this personality and so on that's going on.

445
00:35:06,747 --> 00:35:09,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Emotions and moods and... And that's satipaṭṭhāna.

446
00:35:10,028 --> 00:35:17,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Satipaṭṭhāna, principle satipaṭṭhāna is impersonal reference through which you will purify everything from your gratuitous sense of personality.

447
00:35:17,490 --> 00:35:21,511
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's for one way for purification of beings, there's nothing else to result in.

448
00:35:22,391 --> 00:35:28,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Stick to satipaṭṭhānas and it will purify beings of personality, i.e.

449
00:35:28,195 --> 00:35:35,159
[SPEAKER_01]: arahantship, in any, in every shape or form, no matter how lingering, how subtle, if you do it sufficiently enough.

450
00:35:35,819 --> 00:35:41,883
[SPEAKER_01]: And ānāpānasati is the fulfillment of four satipaṭṭhānas, nothing else.

451
00:35:43,504 --> 00:35:44,745
[SPEAKER_00]: That's ānāpānasati sutta.

